{
  "dom_id": "step-q34672",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14214993/assets/b8a944368cb4549780c5868c164a57d55fffff205ef0816b7316cb9fcf5a93dc.png)\n\nGiven the curve $y=f(x)$ and the curve $y=f(x+a)$, which of the following is the value of $a?$",
  "worked_solution": "Every point on the graph of $y=f(x+a)$ is $2$ units *to the left* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$ So the graph of $y=f(x+a)$ is a horizontal translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2$ units to the *left*. \n\nConsequently, $y=f(x+a)=f(x+2)$ and so $a=2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
